The selection criteria specifically required maternal urine testing, which is a relatively narrow focus. Most Down's 
syndrome screening research uses maternal serum markers or ultrasound, explaining the high exclusion rate (92%). The 8 
included studies represent the core literature on urine-based screening, primarily focusing on beta-core fragment and 
free beta-hCG in urine as potential screening markers. All included studies had appropriate reference standards 
(chromosomal verification via amniocentesis or karyotyping).

