      "task_outcome_detailed": "A total of 100 studies (indices 201-300) were screened against the protocol for 'Urine  
  tests for Down's syndrome screening'. Selection criteria required studies evaluating tests of maternal urine in       
  women up to 24 weeks of gestation for Down's syndrome, compared with a reference standard. 5 studies met these        
  criteria (Indices: 202, 204, 277, 297, 300). Study 202 evaluated urinary free β-hCG and β-core hCG in 163 control,    
  13 Down syndrome, and 5 trisomy 18 pregnancies from 8-25 weeks. Study 204 evaluated urinary hyperglycosylated hCG in  
  first trimester with 8 Down syndrome cases and 55 controls. Study 277 measured urinary gonadotropin peptide in 14     
  Down syndrome cases and 91 controls at 17-21 weeks. Study 297 was a prospective study of urinary beta-core fragment   
  with 23 Down syndrome cases. Study 300 evaluated UGP in 29 Down syndrome pregnancies and 297 controls in Asian        
  women. The remaining 95 studies were excluded primarily because they evaluated serum markers (most common),           
  ultrasound markers (e.g., nuchal translucency), or did not involve urine testing for Down's syndrome.",               
      "additional_context": "Most excluded studies focused on maternal serum biochemistry (e.g., AFP, hCG, PAPP-A,      
  inhibin A) or ultrasound markers (e.g., nuchal translucency, ductus venosus, umbilical cord diameter). Studies        
  establishing reference ranges in healthy pregnancies without DS case validation (e.g., Study 241) were also           
  excluded. Study 283 was excluded as it was a modeling/simulation study rather than an actual evaluation of urine      
  tests. The conservative approach was applied where studies had DS cases but unclear reference standards - these were  
  included with medium confidence."                                                                                     
