│     "abstract": "OBJECTIVE Our purpose was to evaluate our experience with a statewide, multiple-marker Down         │
│ syndrome screening program. STUDY DESIGN The results of 18,712 screening tests performed from July 1, 1991, to Oct.  │
│ 31, 1992, were reviewed. Amniocentesis and aneuploidy detection rates were compared with the experience of a         │
│ previous year (1989-1990) in which material serum alpha-fetoprotein was used for detection of Down syndrome. RESULTS │
│ Positive screening tests (Down syndrome risk > or = 1/190) occurred in 665 of 18,712 (3.5%) patients; 516 of 665     │
│ (78%) patients accepted amniocentesis. Fifteen aneuploidies were identified: 12 trisomy 21, one trisomy 18, one      │
│ trisomy 13, and one 48,XXXY. The overall detection rate was one in 34 amniocenteses performed; for trisomy 21 it was │
│ one in 43. In a previous year in which maternal serum alpha-fetoprotein alone was used, 3.6% had positive screening  │
│ tests (Down syndrome risk > or = 270); the detection rate for all aneuploidies was one in 57 amniocenteses, and for  │
│ trisomy 21 it was one in 114. The expanded maternal serum alpha-fetoprotein test was well accepted by clinicians,    │
│ with 36% of gravid state residents undergoing screening. CONCLUSION The multiple marker test is a good screening     │
│ tool and is superior to material serum alpha-fetoprotein alone."                                                     │
