│     "title": "Aneuploidy screening: ultrasound versus biochemistry",                                                 │
│     "abstract": "In the past 15\u201320 years, many maternal serum and ultrasound markers have been proposed for     │
│ aneuploidy screening in the second and first trimesters. This article is a review of the multiple modalities of      │
│ screening for chromosomal anomalies proposed to date, their performance and practicability. With regard to the       │
│ dilemma of choosing biochemical or ultrasound screening, the results of large series published to date have been     │
│ much more consistently in favor of serum markers, while a few single ultrasound first-trimester markers exhibit, for │
│ some research groups with specially trained sonographers and quality control, identical or better performance than   │
│ the combination of multiple biochemical markers. Second-trimester Down's syndrome screening with biochemical markers │
│ is now well established and widely accepted. First-trimester screening represents an obvious advantage for patients  │
│ and practitioners. The combination of serum and ultrasound markers, in the first trimester of pregnancy, is still    │
│ controversial and subject to discussion and research. Our experience of more than 13 years of prenatal screening in  │
│ a public hospital, and co-ordinating a multicenter project in Girona (Spain) is presented. A non-interventional      │
│ trial in which 3139 patients have undergone both first- and second-trimester screening is reported. In our series, a │
│ first-trimester protocol combining maternal age, biochemical markers and nuchal translucency shows superior          │
│ performance in aneuploidy screening.Taylor & Francis Online"                                                         │
